section: platform
title: The Palette platform
about: |
  What Palette itself does with a build: the validate routes it has and does not call, the
  states that hide an error, the webhook of the pivot, and the answers of its API.
rules:
- id: palette-validate
  title: The validate route of Palette for the cloud type passes
  engines:
  - aws
  - eks
  - gcp
  - gke
  - azure
  - aks
  - maas
  - edge
  - vsphere
  tier: palette
  severity: fail
  status: seen
  what: |
    `POST /v1/spectroclusters/{cloudType}/validate` takes the body of the create. It checks the
    name, the credit, the profile packs and their variables, and the machine resources against
    the needs of the packs. For GKE it checks the pool size. Only the console calls it; the
    provider and palette-cli do not, and the create path of the API runs the name and the limit
    checks alone. A 4xx is one refusal, with its code; a 200 lists the errors of each pool and
    each profile. On EKS the route reports no CPU floor for a managed node group. One `t3.xlarge`
    worker got `200` with no error; the aws route answers `MachinePoolWithMinCpuRequired` for the
    same pool. No run shows whether the EKS create fails later. `worker-cpu-floor`, `pack-dependency`,
    and `ecr-oci-images` call the same route and read one class of its errors each; this rule
    reads them all.
  check:
    kind: palette
    method: POST
    path: /v1/spectroclusters/{{ .routeCloud }}/validate
    body: .validateBody
    expr: |
      .response as $r
      | if ($r.ok | not) then ["Palette refused the entity: \($r.body.code // $r.status): \($r.body.message // ($r.body | tostring))"]
        else [ ($r.body.profiles // [])[] | .uid as $u | (.packs.results // [])[] | .name as $n | (.errors // [])[] | "profile \($u), pack \($n): \(.code): \(.message)" ]
           + [ ($r.body.machinePools.results // [])[] | .name as $n | (.errors // [])[] | "pool \($n): \(.code): \(.message)" ]
        end
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Run the validate route at the start of each create, and add it to the SDK and the provider.
  evidence:
  - kind: run
    ref: a live run
  - kind: source
    note: the validate route of a cluster checks the profiles of the cluster
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/gke/validate-refused.json
    clean: fixtures/gke/clean.json
  - fault: fixtures/eks/validate-cpu.json
- id: status-not-cloud
  title: The status shows an error when the cloud refuses the build
  engines:
  - gke
  - gcp
  tier: watch
  severity: fail
  status: seen
  what: |
    GCP refuses the build, and the status of the cluster shows no error: the GCP provider
    reports nothing to the status, so `ControlPlaneNodeAdditionDone` is `True`. The
    refusal is in the events alone, as `GCPManagedControlPlaneReconcile` with the message of GCP,
    or `ReconcileError`. A stop of CAPG after a pool change, "machinePoolList length (1) !=
    managedMachinePoolList length (2)", comes in the same event and shows in no condition.
  check:
    kind: watch
    expr: |
      [ (.events // [])[]
        | select(.severity == "Warning" or .severity == "Error")
        | select((.reason // "") | test("Reconcile"))
        | select((.message // "") | test("InvalidArgument|PERMISSION_DENIED|has not been used in project|is disabled|QUOTA_EXCEEDED|NOT_FOUND|failed to (create|reconcile)|machinePoolList length"))
        | "event \(.reason) at \(.metadata.creationTimestamp): \(.message | .[:240])" ]
      | unique | .[:3]
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Copy an error the provider gets from the cloud into a condition of the cluster.
  evidence:
  - kind: run
    ref: '#90'
    note: 193 GCPManagedControlPlaneReconcile events with the refusal of GCP, and no error in the status; after the pool change, the CAPG stop in the same event
  - kind: source
    note: Palette puts the refusal of the cloud in the status of the cluster
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/gke/pool-roll-mismatch.json
    clean: fixtures/gke/watch-running.json
- id: infrastructure-stalled
  title: The infrastructure of the cluster is ready within 30 minutes, or the status says why
  engines:
  - all
  tier: watch
  severity: fail
  status: seen
  what: |
    A build waits at `CloudInfrastructureReady: False` with the reason `PreparingInfrastructure`
    for 30 minutes or more, and the status shows no error. In #90 the Kubernetes Engine API was
    off, and only an event of the cluster named it. The watch stops here and names the
    condition, so a person reads the events and the audit log of the cloud, and does not wait
    for the timeout of the client.
  check:
    kind: watch
    expr: |
      (.cluster.status.state // "") as $s | (.elapsed // 0) as $e
      | if ($s != "Provisioning" and $s != "Pending") or $e < 1800 then []
        else [ (.cluster.status.conditions // [])[]
               | select(.type == "CloudInfrastructureReady" and (.status // "") == "False")
               | "the cluster is \($s) and CloudInfrastructureReady is False with the reason \(.reason // "?") after \($e)s, and the status shows no error: read the events of the cluster and the audit log of the cloud" ]
        end
  reproduce:
  - '{{ palette_curl }} "${PALETTE_API:-https://api.spectrocloud.com}/v1/events/components/spectrocluster/{{ .clusterUid // "<cluster uid>" }}?limit=50" | jq ''.items[] | {reason, severity, message: .message[:160]}'''
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Copy an error the provider gets from the cloud into the condition, and fail the build after a limit.
  evidence:
  - kind: run
    ref: '#90'
    note: PreparingInfrastructure for about 30 minutes with the Kubernetes Engine API off, and no error in the status
  - kind: run
    ref: a live run
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/gke/infrastructure-stalled.json
    clean: fixtures/gke/watch-running.json
